winemac: read the displays again on activation only when they changed

Every time a Wine program becomes the active app, winemac sends it a "displays changed" event,
in case a display change was missed while it was in the background (another Wine program that
had changed the resolution may have died). win32u answers by reading every display again: all
modes, EDID over IOKit, the GPUs, and it deletes and rewrites the display registry keys through
the server. That runs on the thread that handles the program's messages. Measured in Genshin
Impact: 48% of a 5-second sample, about 2.4 seconds, at each switch back to the game.

winemac now keeps the displays as Wine was last told about them (ids, places, sizes, modes,
refresh rates) and skips the event on activation when they are the same. A real change still
arrives through the reconfiguration callback, which also runs in the background, and an
activation after a change that was somehow missed still sends the event, because the state
differs. FINEWINE_DISPLAY_RESYNC=always brings the old behaviour back.

tests/runtime/idle-window.c with activate.swift: three activations read the displays once
(the first), where they read them three times before.

--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.h
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.h
@@ -93,6 +93,7 @@
     NSImage* applicationIcon;
 
     BOOL beenActive;
+    NSString* finewineDisplayState; /* the displays as Wine was last told about them */
 
     NSMutableSet* windowsBeingDragged;
 }
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
@@ -1221,10 +1221,55 @@
         [self adjustWindowLevels];
     }
 
+    /* FineWine: the displays, their places, sizes and modes, as one string to compare. */
+    - (NSString*) finewineCurrentDisplayState
+    {
+        CGDirectDisplayID displays[32];
+        uint32_t count = 0, i;
+        NSMutableString* state = [NSMutableString string];
+
+        if (CGGetOnlineDisplayList(sizeof(displays) / sizeof(displays[0]), displays, &count) != kCGErrorSuccess)
+            return nil;
+        for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
+        {
+            CGRect bounds = CGDisplayBounds(displays[i]);
+            CGDisplayModeRef mode = CGDisplayCopyDisplayMode(displays[i]);
+            [state appendFormat:@"%u:%d,%d,%d,%g,%g,%g,%g,%zu,%zu,%g,%u;", (unsigned int)displays[i],
+                   (int)CGDisplayIsActive(displays[i]), (int)CGDisplayIsMain(displays[i]),
+                   (int)CGDisplayMirrorsDisplay(displays[i]),
+                   bounds.origin.x, bounds.origin.y, bounds.size.width, bounds.size.height,
+                   mode ? CGDisplayModeGetPixelWidth(mode) : 0, mode ? CGDisplayModeGetPixelHeight(mode) : 0,
+                   mode ? CGDisplayModeGetRefreshRate(mode) : 0.0, mode ? (unsigned int)CGDisplayModeGetIOFlags(mode) : 0];
+            if (mode) CGDisplayModeRelease(mode);
+        }
+        return state;
+    }
+
     - (void) sendDisplaysChanged:(BOOL)activating
     {
         macdrv_event* event;
         WineEventQueue* queue;
+
+        /* FineWine: becoming active sends this so that Wine catches up with a display change it
+         * may have missed (see applicationDidBecomeActive:). Wine answers by reading every
+         * display again, all modes, EDID and GPU, and rewriting its registry keys, on the
+         * thread that handles the program's messages: 2.4 seconds measured in a game, at every
+         * switch back to it. When the displays are as Wine was last told, there is nothing to
+         * catch up with. FINEWINE_DISPLAY_RESYNC=always brings the old behaviour back. */
+        {
+            static int always = -1;
+            NSString* state = [self finewineCurrentDisplayState];
+            if (always < 0)
+            {
+                const char* env = getenv("FINEWINE_DISPLAY_RESYNC");
+                always = env && !strcmp(env, "always");
+            }
+            if (activating && !always && state && finewineDisplayState &&
+                [state isEqualToString:finewineDisplayState])
+                return;
+            [finewineDisplayState release];
+            finewineDisplayState = [state copy];
+        }
 
         event = macdrv_create_event(DISPLAYS_CHANGED, nil);
         event->displays_changed.activating = activating;
